Got you: grounded Nicolas Cage ridiculousness. The world stays normal; Cage is the unstable variable.

Nicolas Cage had been driving through Virginia for approximately forty-seven minutes when he decided that he was being followed.

Not by a government agency.

Not by the Freemasons.

Not even by a particularly determined fan.

By a gray Toyota Sienna.

The Sienna had been behind him since the last gas station.

Cage checked the mirror.

Still there.

He changed lanes.

The Sienna changed lanes.

He slowed down.

The Sienna slowed down.

Cage narrowed his eyes.

“Interesting.”

His passenger, a half-eaten bag of beef jerky, offered no opinion.

Cage accelerated.

The Sienna accelerated.

“Oh, you wanna dance.”

The Toyota Sienna did not want to dance.

It contained a family of five from Ohio going to the Outer Banks.

But Nicolas Cage did not know that.

Ten minutes later, he pulled into a Virginia Welcome Center.

The Sienna followed.

Cage parked.

The Sienna parked three spaces away.

A father climbed out wearing cargo shorts and began helping a six-year-old find his Crocs.

Cage remained motionless.

“A family,” he whispered.

He looked at the jerky.

“Perfect cover.”

Inside the welcome center, Cage wandered past the brochures.

Williamsburg.

Virginia Beach.

Shenandoah.

Historic Jamestown.

Then he saw it.

A brochure titled:

VIRGINIA: WHERE HISTORY LIVES

Cage froze.

“Lives?”

He picked it up.

A cheerful elderly volunteer behind the desk said, “You can take that.”

Cage slowly turned.

“For free?”

“Yes.”

“No oath?”

“…No.”

“No membership?”

“No, sir.”

Cage approached the desk.

“Ma’am. I need you to answer one question very carefully.”

The volunteer lowered her crossword puzzle.

“Okay.”

“When this brochure says history lives…”

She waited.

“…is that metaphorical?”

“Yes.”

Cage relaxed.

“Good.”

He took two brochures.

“Because if Washington is back, I have several follow-up questions.”

The volunteer stared at him.

Cage stared back.

Then she squinted.

“Aren’t you Nicolas Cage?”

He looked around.

“That depends.”

“On what?”

“Why you’re asking.”

“My grandson loves National Treasure.”

Cage leaned over the counter.

“Your grandson has excellent instincts.”

She laughed.

“You looking for treasure?”

“No.”

Cage paused.

“Unless you know something.”

“I know there’s a vending machine around the corner.”

Cage’s eyes narrowed.

“What kind?”

“Snacks.”

“Of course.”

He walked to the vending machine.

Nothing unusual.

Doritos.

M&Ms.

Peanut butter crackers.

Then Cage noticed that item B4 was empty.

He stepped closer.

“B… four.”

A man filling his coffee nearby looked over.

Cage whispered:

“Before.”

The man immediately walked away.

Cage pulled out his phone.

B4.

Before.

What came before Virginia?

He searched his own memory.

The colonies.

England.

Rome?

Dinosaurs?

He stopped himself.

“No. Too deep.”

This was exactly how these things got out of control.

He returned to the desk.

“Ma’am.”

“Yes?”

“Why is B4 empty?”

She looked toward the vending machine.

“Probably sold out.”

“Of what?”

She shrugged.

“Peanut M&Ms, maybe.”

Cage absorbed this.

Peanuts.

Virginia was famous for peanuts.

He looked at the brochure.

Virginia.

Peanuts.

B4.

Before.

His breathing quickened.

Then a little boy walked past him holding a pack of peanut M&Ms.

Cage pointed.

“You.”

The boy stopped.

His father stopped.

Everyone stopped.

“Where did you get those?”

The boy pointed toward the gift shop.

Cage turned.

There was an entire shelf of them.

Silence.

Cage slowly lowered his hand.

The father said, “You okay, man?”

Cage stared at the shelf.

Then at the vending machine.

Then at the brochure.

Then, finally, at himself reflected in the glass refrigerator door.

He nodded.

“I almost made a movie out of that.”

The father blinked.

“Aren’t you Nicolas Cage?”

Cage sighed.

“That is increasingly becoming the consensus.”

Outside, the gray Toyota Sienna pulled away.

Cage watched it leave.

The elderly volunteer stepped outside behind him.

“There goes your secret family.”

Cage looked at her.

“You knew?”

“They asked me where the bathrooms were.”

Cage nodded slowly.

“So you were part of it.”

She laughed.

He smiled.

“Good one.”

Cage climbed back into his car, placed the Virginia brochure carefully on the passenger seat, and started the engine.

He drove approximately eleven miles.

Then glanced over at the brochure.

WHERE HISTORY LIVES.

He shook his head.

“No.”

Another mile passed.

“Nope.”

Two more miles.

He looked at it again.

“…Although Jamestown is weird.”

The brochure said nothing.

Cage pointed at it.

“Don’t start.”

That’s about the Cage recursion governor I’d use: ordinary event → Cage finds a pattern → almost constructs a conspiracy → reality swats him gently back down → one tiny unresolved hook survives.